                <pb facs="0279" n="177" sameAs="frus:frus1943/pg177"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>No official record of the substance of this meeting has been found, but William
                    Phillips gives an account of the conversation in his volume of memoirs <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ventures in Diplomacy</hi> (Boston: The Beacon Press,
                    1952), pp. 389–390. According to this account, Roosevelt asked Phillips to see
                    Churchill and give him his frank impressions of conditions in India. Churchill
                    apparently took strong exception to Phillips’ proposal that Indian leaders be
                    given a measure of authority to deal with domestic affairs. Phillips also
                    relates that he immediately reported upon his private talk with Churchill to
                    Roosevelt during a luncheon conversation with the President.</p>
            </div>
